# G046 — Small Web Server

*v2. See `web-server.md` for prose: the other side of the bilateral
protocol, routing as `match` applied to URLs, request-response as
the universal protocol shape, static file serving as cached
resolution, access log as the server observing itself.*

---

## Choreographic Case: dpn-api-client Pattern

[api-server @port
    IN ORDER TO
        expose database read/write/refresh as HTTP routes
        where each route is a capability endpoint the Ghost Registry mirrors
        and every request is logged by the server observing itself

    @server <- [web-server[new {name: "dpn-api", port: @port}]]

    [web-server[route
                 {server: @server,
                  method: "GET",  path: "/health",
                  handler: [health-check]}]]
    [web-server[route
                 {server: @server,
                  method: "POST", path: "/read",
                  handler: [db-read]}]]
    [web-server[route
                 {server: @server,
                  method: "POST", path: "/write",
                  handler: [db-write]}]]
    [web-server[route
                 {server: @server,
                  method: "POST", path: "/refresh",
                  handler: [refresh-handler]}]]

    [web-server[start {server: @server}]]

    every 1 minute
        @log <- [web-server[access-log
                              {server: @server,
                               since: [clock[now]] - 60000}]]
        @log
            where 5xx count is greater than 0
                -> [alert[dispatch
                           {kind: "server-errors",
                            count: @log:5xx-count}]]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[web-server
    new                 {name, port}
    route               {server, method, path, handler}
    serve-static        {server, path, content, content-type}
    start               {server}
    stop                {server}
    access-log          {server, since}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: route table as `where` dispatch

[web-server[dispatch {server: @s, request: @req}]
    IN ORDER TO
        match @req's method and path to a registered handler
        or return 404 if none claims it

    @s:routes
        where route:method is @req:method
        and   route:path   is @req:path
            -> invoke route:handler with @req; return its Response
        otherwise
            -> return 404 Not Found
            -> append #route-miss to access-log
]

Same shape InnateScript's resolver uses for `@reference` resolution:
each entry maps an identifier (URL path / reference name) to a
handler. URL routing and reference resolution are the same algorithm
expressed in two syntaxes.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: access log as observer within the server

[web-server[handle {server: @s, request: @req}]
    record @req:ts, method, path as open log entry
    dispatch @req via routes
    complete log entry with status, elapsed-ms
    IN ORDER TO
        make the server auditable by itself
        so the access log IS a G040-style sniffer running inside the listener
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    @srv <- [web-server[new {name: "demo", port: 8080}]]
    [web-server[route
                 {server: @srv, method: "GET", path: "/health",
                  handler: [lambda [_] {status: 200, body: "ok"}]}]]

    [web-server[serve-static
                 {server: @srv, path: "/style.css",
                  content: "body { background: #0A0A0F; }",
                  content-type: "text/css"}]]

    [web-server[start {server: @srv}]]
    ;; GET /health → 200 ok
    ;; GET /style.css → 200 body { background: #0A0A0F; }
    ;; GET /missing → 404
]
